# Contract-major 3 ES256 grants and proofs

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Sign a compute-job grant and proof with P-256. Convert DER signatures to low-S raw form,
verify the envelope, and refuse high-S signatures, another holder, and cross-major inputs.

The signer owns conversion and normalization; assembly refuses noncanonical signatures.
The host supplies trusted public keys and context and owns replay, revocation, and the decision.
The generated keys stay in memory; this notebook starts no services and creates no files.

[Open this 1.1.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.1.0%2Fdocs%2Fnotebooks%2Fes256-signing.livemd).
The launch opens this journey at its immutable release tag.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.1.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate P-256 keys and convert signatures

OTP returns DER ECDSA signatures. Convert to raw `r || s`, normalize low S, and only then
assemble a compact JWS. The holder thumbprint uses the EC JWK construction.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.Es256
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V3
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V3.EcJwk

{es_issuer_public, es_issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:ecdh, :prime256v1)
{es_holder_public, es_holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:ecdh, :prime256v1)

es256_sign = fn message, private_key ->
  der = :crypto.sign(:ecdsa, :sha256, message, [private_key, :prime256v1])
  {:ok, raw} = Es256.der_to_raw(der)
  {:ok, low_s} = Es256.normalize_low_s(raw)
  low_s
end

{:ok, es_holder_thumbprint} = EcJwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(es_holder_public, %{})

now = 1_800_001_000
:es256_signer_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:es256_signer_ready
```

## Build and sign the grant

The issuer permits a batch job using at most eight GPU hours. Contract-major 3 has its own
producers and inherits the range selectors of contract-major 2.

```elixir
es_grant = %V3.Grant{
  key_id: "es-issuer-1",
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "grant-2026-es-001",
  audiences: ["https://service.example"],
  issued_at: 1_800_000_000,
  not_before: 1_800_000_000,
  expires_at: 1_800_003_600,
  holder_thumbprint: es_holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [
    %V3.Operation{
      name: "start_job",
      selectors: [
        {:equals, ["job", "queue"], {:string, "batch"}},
        {:lte, ["job", "gpu_hours"], {:integer, 8}}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{:ok, es_grant_input} = V3.grant_signing_input(es_grant, %{})

{:ok, es_grant_compact} =
  V3.assemble_compact(es_grant_input, es256_sign.(es_grant_input.message, es_issuer_private))

:grant_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:grant_signed
```

## Build and sign the proof

Bind the job arguments and the exact grant bytes to this invocation. Only the low-S raw
signature enters assembly.

```elixir
es_arguments =
  {:object, [{"job", {:object, [{"queue", {:string, "batch"}}, {"gpu_hours", {:integer, 4}}]}}]}

es_proof = %V3.Proof{
  holder_public_key: es_holder_public,
  proof_id: "proof-2026-es-001",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://service.example/jobs",
  issued_at: now,
  nonce: nil,
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120004",
  operation: "start_job",
  grant_compact: es_grant_compact,
  cast_arguments: es_arguments
}

{:ok, es_proof_input} = V3.proof_signing_input(es_proof, %{})
es_proof_signature = es256_sign.(es_proof_input.message, es_holder_private)
{:ok, es_proof_compact} = V3.assemble_compact(es_proof_input, es_proof_signature)

:proof_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:proof_signed
```

## Supply trusted keys and expected context

Use the shared expected-context structs from the contract-major 1 namespace. Select the
contract-major 3 verifier explicitly; never probe profiles until one accepts.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Credentials, ExpectedGrant, ExpectedRequest, TrustedIssuer}

es_trusted = %TrustedIssuer{key_id: "es-issuer-1", public_key: es_issuer_public}
es_credentials = %Credentials{grant: es_grant_compact, proof: es_proof_compact}

es_expected = %ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: es_trusted,
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://service.example",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://service.example/jobs",
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120004",
  operation: "start_job",
  cast_arguments: es_arguments,
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: :not_required,
  bounds: %{}
}

expected_grant = %ExpectedGrant{
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://service.example",
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  bounds: %{}
}

:expected_context_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:expected_context_ready
```

## Verify the grant and envelope

Both verifiers use only public keys. Show the version and operation while keeping random
signatures and thumbprints out of persisted output.

```elixir
{:ok, es_grant_facts} = V3.verify_grant(es_grant_compact, es_trusted, expected_grant)
{:ok, es_envelope_facts} = V3.check_envelope(es_credentials, es_expected)

%{
  grant: Map.take(es_grant_facts, [:version, :grant_id, :authorization]),
  envelope: Map.take(es_envelope_facts, [:operation, :authorization])
}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  grant: %{version: 3, grant_id: "grant-2026-es-001", authorization: :not_evaluated},
  envelope: %{operation: "start_job", authorization: :not_evaluated}
}
```

## Refuse the high-S twin

ECDSA also verifies `(r, n - s)` in the backend. Demonstrate that fact, recover the original
low-S signature, and show that assembly and envelope verification both refuse the high-S twin.

```elixir
# The P-256 group order (contract-major 3 specification § 3.2):
n = 0xFFFFFFFF00000000FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFBCE6FAADA7179E84F3B9CAC2FC632551

<<es_r::256, es_s::256>> = es_proof_signature
high_s_signature = <<es_r::256, n - es_s::256>>

high_s_proof =
  es_proof_input.message <> "." <> Base.url_encode64(high_s_signature, padding: false)

# The backend verifies DER, so frame the twin as a DER ECDSA-Sig-Value
# (SEQUENCE { INTEGER r, INTEGER s }; a leading zero byte keeps an INTEGER positive):
der_integer = fn integer ->
  bytes = :binary.encode_unsigned(integer)
  bytes = if :binary.first(bytes) >= 0x80, do: <<0>> <> bytes, else: bytes
  <<0x02, byte_size(bytes)>> <> bytes
end

high_s_body = der_integer.(es_r) <> der_integer.(n - es_s)
high_s_der = <<0x30, byte_size(high_s_body)>> <> high_s_body

true =
  :crypto.verify(:ecdsa, :sha256, es_proof_input.message, high_s_der, [
    es_holder_public,
    :prime256v1
  ])

{:ok, raw_twin} = Es256.der_to_raw(high_s_der)
{:ok, ^es_proof_signature} = Es256.normalize_low_s(raw_twin)
{:error, :invalid} = V3.assemble_compact(es_proof_input, high_s_signature)

{:error, :invalid} =
  V3.check_envelope(%{es_credentials | proof: high_s_proof}, es_expected)

:high_s_refused
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:high_s_refused
```

## Refuse another holder

This proof has a valid P-256 signature of its own, but the grant names a different holder
thumbprint. Signature validity cannot replace holder binding.

```elixir
{other_public, other_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:ecdh, :prime256v1)
{:ok, other_input} = V3.proof_signing_input(%{es_proof | holder_public_key: other_public}, %{})

{:ok, other_proof} =
  V3.assemble_compact(other_input, es256_sign.(other_input.message, other_private))

{:error, :invalid} =
  V3.check_envelope(%{es_credentials | proof: other_proof}, es_expected)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse cross-major bytes

First decode these bytes under their own contract-major. Contract-majors 1 and 2 must refuse
both the grant and proof without attempting a fallback.

```elixir
{:ok, _grant} = V3.decode_grant(es_grant_compact, %{})
{:ok, _proof} = V3.decode_proof(es_proof_compact, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.decode_grant(es_grant_compact, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.decode_proof(es_proof_compact, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V2.decode_grant(es_grant_compact, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V2.decode_proof(es_proof_compact, %{})

[:major_one_grant, :major_one_proof, :major_two_grant, :major_two_proof]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:major_one_grant, :major_one_proof, :major_two_grant, :major_two_proof]
```

See [choosing a profile](../guides/choosing-a-profile.md) and the
[ES256 specification](../../spec/bap-v3.md) for the suite and canonical signature rules.
